155 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E (Chord-Scale Theory)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
156 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E (One of the most common topics within Jazz pedagogy is chord-scale theory - the study of which scales should be played over each different type of chords. For example, we often learn to use the Lydian mode over a major chord, or the Dorian mode over a minor scale, different options for dominant chords \(Mixolydian, or Altered, or octatonic, etc\). This knowledge is important, since we must address the changes \(chords\) correctly when improvising melodies, just like having the correct alphabet for each type of chord. Modern jazz generally features complex harmonies, making it very hard to play intuitively by ear, so knowing the scales and the different options for each type of chord is extremely important. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
157 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E (However, chord-scale theory can very often become a hindrance, as it forces a vertical thinking \(thinking about each individual chord\) as opposed to a horizontal thinking \(melodies flowing seamlessly threading between chords\). The excess of a chord-scale approach can lead to a "boxed" improvisation, overly compartmentalized, in which there's no sense of naturalness in the melody. All notes used may be correct, but the improvisation doesn't sound organic and fluent. It requires a mental re-booth every time you move to a new chord.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
158 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E (Because of these issues, we would like to propose a new way to think about chord scale theory - one that prioritizes fluency, motivic development and lyricism. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
